## Summary

Plan, write, and publish the first long-form engineering case study derived from the framework repository and its public evidence.

Working title:

> From Repository-Specific Agent Workflows to a Reusable Engineering Framework

The article should explain how a repository-specific agent-assisted development workflow evolved into a reusable framework, which concerns proved generalisable, which remained repository-specific, and how the resulting system constrains agent behaviour through repository contracts, task shaping, runtime boundaries, deterministic validation, and explicit human review.

The article must be evidence-led. It should use the repository as the primary technical source and link readers to public code, tests, documentation, examples, tasks, and evaluation artefacts.

This issue covers article planning, source-controlled drafting, evidence review, publication, and repository cross-linking. It does not require the article to be implemented before its evidence dependencies are complete.

## Dependencies

The final article depends on the implemented and reviewed outputs of:

- #2 — deterministic framework self-validation;
- #3 — deterministic offline launcher tests;
- #4 — architecture, evolution, and public evidence-boundary documentation;
- #5 — reproducible reference repository fixture and adoption walkthrough;
- #6 — evaluation harness and calibration evidence.

Drafting may begin before all dependencies are complete, but:

- technical claims must not be finalised against planned behaviour;
- links must target implemented public artefacts;
- evaluation conclusions must not be written before the calibration evidence exists;
- publication must not occur while material evidence dependencies remain unresolved.

## Purpose

The article should make the framework legible to an experienced engineer who is unlikely to inspect an unfamiliar repository without first understanding why the work matters.

It should establish that:

- the framework arose from repeated operational use rather than speculative prompt design;
- coding-agent reliability depends on repository and delivery systems, not only model capability;
- the framework standardises control surfaces while preserving repository-specific domain and architecture knowledge;
- runtime permissions, validation, scope control, and human judgement are first-class design concerns;
- public claims are tied to inspectable evidence and clearly separated from private professional experience;
- the current evidence has meaningful limitations.

The article is not intended to promote coding agents generally or claim that the framework has conclusively improved productivity.

## Intended audience

Primary audience:

- Staff and Principal software engineers;
- AI platform and agent-infrastructure engineers;
- engineering leaders responsible for introducing coding agents safely;
- hiring managers evaluating applied AI developer-tooling experience.

Assume readers:

- understand normal software-delivery practices;
- are familiar with coding agents at a high level;
- do not need a general explanation of LLMs;
- care more about architecture, controls, trade-offs, and evidence than product advocacy.

## Central claim

The article should defend a narrow central claim:

> A useful coding-agent framework is not primarily a collection of prompts. It is an engineering control system built around repository-specific context, bounded tasks, runtime permissions, deterministic validation, and explicit human decision points.

Supporting claims must be linked to public evidence or labelled clearly as private professional experience.

## Required structure

The exact headings may change during drafting, but the article should cover the following narrative.

### 1. The operational problem

Explain concrete limitations encountered when coding agents are used as isolated conversational tools:

- repository context is repeatedly reconstructed;
- agents may broaden a task or make silent product and architecture decisions;
- validation selection is inconsistent;
- permissions may exceed task requirements;
- review handoffs omit evidence or limitations;
- lessons from failures remain session-local;
- existing repositories differ too much for one universal prompt.

Keep this section grounded in engineering failure modes rather than broad predictions about AI and software development.

### 2. The first repository-specific workflow

Describe the origin in a substantial private personal application at a level safe for public discussion.

Relevant concerns may include:

- issue-led work;
- repository-specific instructions;
- standard command and container boundaries;
- scoped GitHub access;
- validation and pull-request handoff;
- protection of security, tenancy, audit, and developer-owned environment boundaries.

Do not imply that the initial implementation was already a reusable framework.

Do not include private code, screenshots, prompts, issue content, schemas, or repository structure.

### 3. What proved reusable

Explain which recurring concerns became framework components.

Include a concise mapping such as:

| Repeated concern | Framework response |
| --- | --- |
| Repository context varies | Repository-specific contract and supporting documentation |
| Tasks contain unresolved decisions | Readiness criteria and discovery boundary |
| Agents improvise commands | Stable command surface over native tooling |
| Permissions are broader than the task | Runtime-specific scoped access |
| Validation evidence is incomplete | Shared validation and PR conventions |
| Existing repositories cannot be replaced wholesale | Incremental adoption tiers |
| Agent tools have different capabilities and risks | Runtime-specific launchers and policy |
| Failures recur unless captured | Feedback into tests, contracts, and tooling |

The final table must match the implemented framework and documentation.

### 4. What deliberately remained repository-specific

Explain why the framework does not attempt to generalise:

- domain invariants;
- architecture boundaries;
- sensitive operations and files;
- native package, build, and deployment tooling;
- validation depth;
- unresolved product and architecture decisions;
- permissions needed for a particular task.

Make the design principle explicit:

> Standardise the control surfaces, not the application architecture.

If this wording no longer matches the reviewed repository documentation, revise it rather than treating it as fixed copy.

### 5. Framework architecture

Summarise the architecture documented by #4.

At minimum, explain the flow between:

```text
Task or issue
    ↓
Repository-specific contract and context
    ↓
Agent runtime and access policy
    ↓
Shared command surface
    ↓
Deterministic validation and CI
    ↓
Human review and merge decision
```

Cover the roles of:

- standing repository policy;
- task-specific instructions;
- runtime-specific prompting and credentials;
- native repository tooling;
- CI and automated checks;
- human approval and review.

Link to the authoritative architecture document rather than reproducing all implementation details.

### 6. Evidence across adoption contexts

Use a sanitised comparison of materially different adoption contexts.

A suitable set is:

- secure document platform;
- mature production commerce application;
- constrained analytics service;
- staged archive platform;
- public reference fixture.

Explain how the primary adaptation concern differed:

| Context | Main adaptation concern |
| --- | --- |
| Secure document platform | Security, tenancy, audit, and scoped credentials |
| Production commerce application | Existing tooling, regional behaviour, and high-risk storefront changes |
| Constrained analytics service | Separation of model interpretation from trusted execution |
| Staged archive platform | Preservation of current behaviour and unresolved roadmap decisions |
| Public fixture | Reproducibility and independently inspectable evidence |

Private adoption examples must be identified as professional experience rather than public proof.

Do not include confidential implementation details or imply that readers can audit private repositories.

### 7. Public reference fixture

Use the result of #5 to demonstrate one independently inspectable framework adoption.

Explain:

- which framework artefacts were inherited;
- which were specialised;
- how native tooling was preserved;
- which controls were deliberately omitted;
- what makes the repository ready for a bounded agent task;
- what the example does and does not prove.

Link to stable repository paths and, where practical, a tagged revision.

### 8. Evaluation and observed limitations

Use the outputs of #6 conservatively.

Cover:

- what the evaluation harness records;
- how deterministic evidence and human adjudication are separated;
- the calibration task;
- observed failures, interventions, or null results;
- which conclusions are supported;
- which effects remain unmeasured.

Do not claim a general productivity improvement from calibration runs.

Do not present one or two runs as causal evidence that the full framework is superior.

### 9. Engineering conclusions

Conclude with a small number of evidence-backed engineering implications.

Candidate conclusions include:

- repository structure and documentation form part of the agent system;
- task quality is an execution control rather than administrative overhead;
- deterministic validation is more reliable than increasingly elaborate prompt prose;
- runtime permissions and credential handling belong in the architecture;
- human review should attach to explicit decision and risk boundaries;
- reusable frameworks should preserve local application knowledge rather than erase it;
- failures should result in durable changes to tests, tooling, or repository contracts where practical.

Only retain conclusions supported by the completed work.

## Evidence map

Create an evidence map before completing the article.

For each substantive technical claim, record:

- claim text or identifier;
- evidence category;
- public source link or private-experience label;
- implementation status;
- whether the source is stable enough to cite;
- any qualification required.

Use the evidence categories defined by #4:

- publicly inspectable evidence;
- private professional experience;
- sanitised derived evidence;
- intentionally excluded material.

The evidence map may live alongside the draft and need not be part of the published article.

The article must not use a planned issue as evidence that functionality exists.

## Article source and publication

The article source must be version controlled.

Preferred final location:

- a personal-site repository containing the published article source.

Acceptable interim location before the site exists:

```text
case-studies/drafts/framework-evolution.md
```

inside this repository, provided it is clearly marked as a draft publication artefact rather than framework documentation.

The published article must have:

- a durable canonical URL;
- readable code and diagram rendering;
- stable links to public evidence;
- publication and last-updated dates;
- a short author description appropriate to the technical context.

Do not make publication depend on a complex bespoke site implementation. A static site is sufficient.

## Diagrams and visual evidence

Include at least one meaningful architecture diagram derived from #4.

A second diagram may show the evolution from repository-specific workflow to reusable framework if it clarifies the abstraction process.

Diagrams should:

- express a control boundary, system flow, or design decision;
- be source controlled where practical;
- remain readable on desktop and mobile;
- avoid decorative AI imagery;
- remain consistent with the repository documentation.

Do not duplicate diagrams unnecessarily between the article and repository. Shared diagram source or explicit derivation is preferable.

## Linking strategy

The article should link to specific public evidence, including where available:

- architecture and evolution documentation;
- public evidence-boundary documentation;
- framework metadata;
- self-validation implementation and tests;
- launcher implementation and tests;
- reference fixture;
- fixture adoption walkthrough;
- calibration task specification;
- selected run records;
- evaluation report;
- relevant issues and merged pull requests.

Prefer stable paths or tagged revisions over links that may change meaning as the default branch evolves.

The framework README should link back to the published article in a concise case-study or further-reading section. The article must not become required reading for setup or contribution.

## Disclosure review

Before publication, perform an explicit disclosure review.

Confirm that the article does not include:

- private source code;
- private prompts or repository instructions;
- private issue or pull-request text;
- credentials or sensitive security implementation details;
- proprietary schemas or business logic;
- customer, commercial, operational, or employee data;
- internal screenshots;
- unapproved company metrics;
- unapproved productivity or adoption figures;
- claims of company-wide or mandatory adoption;
- material whose ownership or publication rights are unclear.

Generic examples derived from private experience must be independently written and must not reproduce private implementation details.

## Style requirements

Write for experienced engineers.

Prefer:

- concrete failure modes;
- system boundaries;
- trade-offs;
- implementation evidence;
- limitations;
- links to primary artefacts;
- direct language.

Avoid:

- recruiter-oriented framing;
- generic definitions of agents or LLMs;
- predictions about developers being replaced;
- broad AI advocacy;
- product-launch language;
- excessive autobiographical narrative;
- unsupported productivity claims;
- claims that the framework makes agent execution safe in an absolute sense;
- inflated descriptions such as “production proven” where the public evidence does not support them.

The article should read as an engineering case study, not a framework tutorial, personal manifesto, or marketing announcement.

## Supporting publication artefacts

Create a short publication summary suitable for a LinkedIn post or similar professional channel.

This summary should:

- state the engineering problem;
- identify the article’s narrow contribution;
- link to the article and repository;
- avoid repeating the article;
- avoid engagement bait, hype, or unsupported claims.

Keep this copy separate from the article source.

A recurring content schedule, newsletter, or broader promotion campaign is not required.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The article has a reviewed title, central claim, intended audience, and outline.
- [ ] Publication waits until the relevant outputs of #2–#6 are implemented and reviewed.
- [ ] The article source is version controlled.
- [ ] An evidence map exists for substantive technical claims.
- [ ] Every claim of implemented or tested public behaviour links to inspectable evidence.
- [ ] Private professional experience is clearly distinguished from publicly verifiable evidence.
- [ ] Planned, implemented, tested, inferred, and measured claims are not conflated.
- [ ] The article explains the operational problem without becoming a general introduction to coding agents.
- [ ] It explains how repository-specific work produced reusable framework abstractions.
- [ ] It explains which concerns deliberately remain repository-specific.
- [ ] It describes the framework architecture and human decision boundaries accurately.
- [ ] It includes a sanitised comparison of different adoption contexts.
- [ ] It links to and explains the public reference fixture.
- [ ] It uses evaluation results conservatively and includes material limitations.
- [ ] It includes at least one genuine failure, intervention, null result, or incorrect assumption where the completed evidence provides one.
- [ ] It contains no unsupported productivity claim.
- [ ] It contains no confidential code, prompts, schemas, screenshots, metrics, or private repository details.
- [ ] At least one meaningful architecture diagram is included.
- [ ] Public links use stable paths or revisions where practical.
- [ ] The article is published at a durable canonical URL.
- [ ] The framework repository links to the published article without making it core setup documentation.
- [ ] A concise publication summary is prepared separately.
- [ ] A final technical, evidence, and disclosure review has been completed.

## Non-goals

This issue does not include:

- publishing the article before its evidence dependencies are complete;
- creating a general explanation of coding agents, RAG, transformers, or LLMs;
- documenting every framework file;
- writing a setup tutorial that duplicates repository documentation;
- comparing coding-agent vendors or models;
- claiming statistically significant evaluation results;
- claiming measured productivity improvement without a separate supported study;
- using pull-request count as the main evidence of value;
- publishing Palace source code or internal implementation details;
- publishing private personal repository content;
- creating a large custom publishing platform;
- establishing a frequent blogging schedule;
- writing multiple derivative articles under this issue;
- optimising the article primarily for search traffic;
- creating a conference talk or video presentation;
- turning the article into career or recruiter copy.

Future articles may cover the issue-to-merge case study or evaluation methodology separately after the underlying evidence is mature.

## Definition of done

An experienced engineer can read the published case study, understand how the framework emerged from repeated repository-specific use, inspect the public evidence supporting its architecture and controls, distinguish that evidence from private professional experience, and see the framework’s limitations without encountering confidential material or claims that exceed the recorded evidence.
